.IX Title "File::Flock::Subprocess 3pm"
.TH File::Flock::Subprocess 3pm 2026-09-16 "perl v5.40.1" "User Contributed Perl Documentation"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
.Vb 1
\& File::Flock::Subprocess \- file locking with flock in a subprocess
.Ve
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 1
\& use File::Flock::Subprocess;
\&
\& lock($filename);
\&
\& lock($filename, \*(Aqshared\*(Aq);
\&
\& lock($filename, undef, \*(Aqnonblocking\*(Aq);
\&
\& lock($filename, \*(Aqshared\*(Aq, \*(Aqnonblocking\*(Aq);
\&
\& unlock($filename);
\&
\& lock_rename($oldfilename, $newfilename)
\&
\& my $lock = new File::Flock \*(Aq/somefile\*(Aq;
\&
\& $lock\->unlock();
\&
\& $lock\->lock_rename(\*(Aq/new/file\*(Aq);
\&
\& forget_locks();
.Ve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
This is a wrapper around File::Flock that starts a subprocess and
does the locking in the subprocess with File::Flock.  The purpose of
this is to handle operating systems (eg: Solaris) that do not retain
locks across a call to \fBfork()\fR.
.PP
The sub-process for this is created with \fBfork()\fR when
File::Flock::Subprocess is compiled.  I've tried to minimize the
side-effects calling \fBfork()\fR by doing calling it early and by using
\&\fBPOSIX::_exit()\fR to quit but it is still worth being aware of.  I suggest
loading File::Flock::Subprocess early.
.PP
Use File::Flock::Forking to automatically detect when this is needed.
.PP
Read the docs for File::Flock for details of the API.
.SH ERRATA
.IX Header "ERRATA"
Any errors reported by the locking proxy File::Flock::Subprocess starts
will be reported as "Compilation Failed" errors because the proxy is
started in a BEGIN{} block.
